<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Manderscheid-Blankenheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1488 CE – 1794 CE (approx.) · capital Blankenheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Eifel county of the counts of Manderscheid, centred on Blankenheim castle at the source of the Ahr.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Manderscheid in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> acquired Blankenheim and its castle by marriage in the 15th century and were raised to counts in 1457. In 1488 their lands were divided, creating the Manderscheid-Blankenheim line alongside other branches such as Manderscheid-Schleiden.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a small, wooded territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> with iron mining and smelting, and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhenish-westphalian-circle">Lower Rhenish-Westphalian Circle</a>. They remained Catholic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-of-manderscheid-blankenheim">Johann of Manderscheid-Blankenheim</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a> from 1569. The later counts gathered a notable library and collection of Roman antiquities at Blankenheim.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The male line died out in 1780, and the lands passed by marriage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sternberg">Sternberg</a> family of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>. French occupation of the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1794 ended the county, which later became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1488 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.31%;width:6.09%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1488 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Blankenheim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Manderscheid, Blankenheim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1641&amp;focus=county-of-manderscheid-blankenheim" aria-label="County of Manderscheid-Blankenheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 426 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Manderscheid-Blankenheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5152 682L5229 694M5152 682L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5152 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5152 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Blankenheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1641&amp;focus=county-of-manderscheid-blankenheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

